MONGODB – DATA MODELLING

1. WHAT IS DATA MODELLING?

Data modelling is the process of deciding how data should be organized,
structured, stored, and connected inside a database.

MongoDB is a document-oriented NoSQL database. It stores data as BSON
documents inside collections.

CORE PRINCIPLE: “Data that is accessed together should ideally be stored
together.”

Example: In a movie application, when a user opens a movie page, the
application may need the movie title, genre, rating, release year, and
cast members. If these details are commonly accessed together, we may
store them in one document.

2. ENTITIES AND ATTRIBUTES

ENTITY: An entity is a distinct object or concept about which an
application stores information.

Examples: - Movie - User - Product - Order - Comment

In MongoDB: - A collection stores documents of a similar type. - Each
document generally represents one instance of an entity.

ATTRIBUTE: An attribute is a property or characteristic that describes
an entity.

Example – Movie entity: - movie_id - title - genre - release_year -
rating - cast

MongoDB represents attributes as field-value pairs inside a document.

Example document:

{ “_id”: 101, “title”: “Inception”, “genre”: “Sci-Fi”, “release_year”:
2010, “rating”: 8.8 }

IMPORTANT TERMS: - Entity = What we want to store. - Collection = A
group of related documents. - Document = An individual record. - Field =
An attribute inside a document. - _id = Unique identifier of a document.

NOTEBOOK DIAGRAM:

        ENTITY: MOVIE
              |
              v
       COLLECTION: movies
              |
              v
       DOCUMENT (one movie)
              |
              v
       FIELDS / ATTRIBUTES
       title, genre, rating, year

3. DATA RELATIONSHIPS

A relationship describes how documents or entities are associated with
one another.

The three common relationship types are: 1. One-to-One (1:1) 2.
One-to-Many (1:N) 3. Many-to-Many (M:N)

MongoDB can represent relationships mainly through: - Embedding -
Referencing

3A. ONE-TO-ONE (1:1)

One document is associated with one other document.

Example: One user has one profile, and each profile belongs to one user.

DIAGRAM:

       USER

(user_id: 101) | | 1 : 1 | PROFILE (profile_id: 501)

EMBEDDING EXAMPLE:

{ “_id”: 101, “name”: “Archit”, “email”: “archit@example.com”,
“profile”: { “city”: “Bangalore”, “occupation”: “Engineer” } }

The profile is stored as a nested document inside the user document.

Use embedding when the profile is usually accessed together with the
user and does not need to be managed independently.

Referencing is also possible if the profile needs to be stored or
managed separately.

3B. ONE-TO-MANY (1:N)

One document is associated with multiple documents.

Example: One movie can have many comments, but each comment belongs to
one movie.

DIAGRAM:

             MOVIE
          (movie_id: 101)
                |
        +-------+-------+
        |       |       |
        v       v       v
     COMMENT COMMENT COMMENT
       1       2       3

OPTION 1 – EMBEDDING:

{ “_id”: 101, “title”: “Inception”, “comments”: [ { “user”: “Archit”,
“text”: “Great movie!” }, { “user”: “Rahul”, “text”: “Amazing movie!” }
] }

Comments are stored inside the movie document.

Use embedding when the number of related records is small or bounded and
they are commonly read with the parent document.

OPTION 2 – REFERENCING:

Movies collection:

{ “_id”: 101, “title”: “Inception” }

Comments collection:

{ “_id”: 501, “movie_id”: 101, “user”: “Archit”, “text”: “Great movie!”
}

The movie_id field connects the comment to its movie.

Use referencing when comments can grow without a practical limit, are
queried independently, or need separate management.

3C. MANY-TO-MANY (M:N)

Multiple documents in one collection can be associated with multiple
documents in another collection.

Example: - One student can enroll in many courses. - One course can have
many students.

DIAGRAM:

  STUDENTS        COURSES
  --------------- ---------
  Archit ————–>   MongoDB
  ————–>          Python

Rahul ————–> MongoDB ————–> SQL

A many-to-many relationship is commonly modelled using references.

OPTION 1 – STORE COURSE IDS IN STUDENT DOCUMENT:

Students collection:

{ “_id”: 101, “name”: “Archit”, “course_ids”: [201, 202] }

Courses collection:

{ “_id”: 201, “course_name”: “MongoDB” }

{ “_id”: 202, “course_name”: “Python” }

OPTION 2 – USE A SEPARATE ENROLLMENTS COLLECTION:

{ “_id”: 501, “student_id”: 101, “course_id”: 201, “enrollment_date”:
“2026-11-21”, “status”: “active” }

A separate relationship collection is useful when the relationship
itself has attributes, such as enrollment date, grade, or status.

4. EMBEDDING

Embedding means storing related information inside the same MongoDB
document using nested documents or arrays.

EXAMPLE:

{ “_id”: 101, “title”: “Inception”, “genre”: “Sci-Fi”, “cast”: [ {
“name”: “Actor One”, “role”: “Cobb” }, { “name”: “Actor Two”, “role”:
“Arthur” } ] }

DIAGRAM:

       MOVIE DOCUMENT
       --------------
       title: Inception
       genre: Sci-Fi
       cast:
         |
         +-- Actor One
         |
         +-- Actor Two

ADVANTAGES: - Related data can be retrieved in one query. - Often
improves read performance when data is accessed together. - Fewer
separate database queries are needed. - Updates to one document are
atomic.

DISADVANTAGES: - Documents can become very large. - Repeated data can
cause duplication. - Updating duplicated information in several
documents can be difficult. - Unbounded arrays can create document
growth problems.

Use embedding when related data is small or bounded and is commonly
accessed with its parent document.

5. REFERENCING

Referencing means storing related data in separate documents and
connecting them using document IDs.

EXAMPLE:

Movies collection:

{ “_id”: 101, “title”: “Inception”, “cast_ids”: [301, 302] }

Actors collection:

{ “_id”: 301, “name”: “Actor One” }

{ “_id”: 302, “name”: “Actor Two” }

DIAGRAM:

       MOVIES COLLECTION
       -----------------
       Inception
       cast_ids: [301, 302]
               |
          REFERENCES
           /     \
          v       v
      ACTOR 301  ACTOR 302
      Actor One  Actor Two

ADVANTAGES: - Reduces unnecessary duplication. - Related data can be
managed independently. - Useful for large or independently queried
datasets. - Helps avoid unbounded growth inside a single document.

DISADVANTAGES: - Retrieving related data may require additional
queries. - Queries can be more complex. - A $lookup may be needed to
combine data from collections.

Use referencing when related data is large, independently accessed,
shared across multiple entities, or changes independently.

6. EMBEDDING VS REFERENCING

EMBEDDING: - Data is stored inside the same document. - Often faster and
simpler when related data is read together. - Can duplicate data. -
Document size and array growth must be controlled. - Best for closely
related, bounded data.

REFERENCING: - Data is stored in separate documents. - Documents are
connected using IDs. - Reduces duplication in many designs. - May
require extra queries or $lookup. - Best for large, independent, shared,
or unbounded data.

IMPORTANT: Referencing is not always better for large datasets, and
embedding is not always faster. The correct choice depends on
application query patterns, data growth, update patterns, and
consistency requirements.

7. HOW TO CHOOSE BETWEEN EMBEDDING AND REFERENCING

Ask these questions while designing a MongoDB schema:

1.  Is the related data usually accessed together?
    -   If yes, consider embedding.
2.  Can the related array grow without a practical limit?
    -   If yes, consider referencing.
3.  Is the related data accessed or updated independently?
    -   If yes, consider referencing.
4.  Is the same information shared by many documents?
    -   Referencing may reduce duplication.
5.  Does the relationship have its own attributes?
    -   Consider a separate relationship collection.

GENERAL RULE: Embed related data when it is bounded and commonly used
with the parent. Reference related data when it is independent, shared,
or potentially unbounded.

  8. MONGODB $lookup
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  $lookup is an aggregation stage used to combine documents from two collections based on matching fields.

It is similar to a LEFT OUTER JOIN in relational databases.

EXAMPLE:

db.movies.aggregate([ { $lookup: { from: “comments”, localField: “_id”,
foreignField: “movie_id”, as: “movie_comments” } }])

EXPLANATION: - from: Collection to join with. - localField: Field from
the current collection. - foreignField: Matching field in the other
collection. - as: Name of the output array containing matching
documents.

In this example: - MongoDB reads documents from the movies collection. -
It matches movies._id with comments.movie_id. - Matching comments are
placed in the movie_comments array.

9. IMPORTANT MONGODB TERMS

Database: A container that holds collections.

Collection: A group of MongoDB documents.

Document: A BSON record stored in a collection.

Field: A key-value pair inside a document.

_id: The unique identifier of a document. MongoDB automatically creates
an ObjectId if an _id is not provided.

Embedded document: A nested document stored inside another document.

Reference: An ID stored in a document to connect it to another document.

$lookup: An aggregation stage used to combine data from collections.

10. QUICK REVISION

1:1 = One user has one profile. 1:N = One movie has many comments. M:N =
Many students enroll in many courses.

Embedding: Store related data inside the same document.

Referencing: Store related data separately and connect it using IDs.

$lookup: Combine related documents from different collections during
aggregation.

MOST IMPORTANT PRINCIPLE: “Data that is accessed together should ideally
be stored together.”

Always design the schema around how the application reads and writes
data, rather than simply copying relational database design into
MongoDB.

